# PIC16F877A ADC LED Dimmer (ADC + PWM)

## Project Description
This project demonstrates how to use the ADC and PWM modules of the PIC16F877A microcontroller to control LED brightness.  
A potentiometer connected to Analog Channel AN0 is used to vary the input voltage. The ADC converts this voltage into a 10-bit digital value, which is then mapped directly to the PWM duty cycle to control LED brightness.

## Objectives
- Configure the ADC module of PIC16F877A
- Read analog voltage using AN0
- Configure PWM using CCP1 module
- Control LED brightness using PWM based on ADC input

## Hardware Components
- PIC16F877A
- Potentiometer (POT-HG)
- LED
- Oscilloscope
- VCC and Ground

## Software Tools
- MPLAB X IDE
- XC8 Compiler
- Proteus (for simulation)

## Working Principle
1. The ADC reads the analog voltage from the potentiometer connected to AN0.
2. The ADC produces a 10-bit digital value (0–1023).
3. This value is passed directly to the PWM module.
4. PWM duty cycle changes according to the ADC value.
5. LED brightness increases or decreases based on the duty cycle.

## Output
- Smooth LED brightness control using analog input
- Real-time response to potentiometer changes

